tools: mkimage: copy the FIT in process instead of shelling out to cp

Rebuilding an existing FIT (-F) starts by making a working copy of it, and
mkimage asks a shell to do that:

	snprintf(cmd, sizeof(cmd), "cp \"%s\" \"%s\"",
		 params->imagefile, tmpfile);

so signing a FIT needs /bin/sh and cp on top of mkimage itself. That rules the
tool out of the minimal images which are exactly where firmware tends to be
assembled; in one of them the failure is

    mkimage: system(cp "u-boot.itb" "u-boot.itb.tmp") failed:
    No such file or directory

which names cp but means there is no shell to run it with: glibc's system()
uses posix_spawn and returns -1 with ENOENT when /bin/sh is missing.

copyfile() already does this in process, lives in tools/fit_common.c and is
already called a few lines further down in this same function to restore the
backup, so use it. Leaving cmd empty makes the existing `if (*cmd && ...)`
guard skip system() altogether, the same way the auto-FIT path above already
does.

No functional change: the temporary file ends up with the same contents.

Signed-off-by: Enrique Hernández Bello <ehbello@gmail.com>
---
--- a/tools/fit_image.c
+++ b/tools/fit_image.c
@@ -960,8 +960,10 @@
 			 MKIMAGE_DTC, params->dtc, tmpfile, params->datafile);
 		debug("Trying to execute \"%s\"\n", cmd);
 	} else {
-		snprintf(cmd, sizeof(cmd), "cp \"%s\" \"%s\"",
-			 params->imagefile, tmpfile);
+		/* Copy the image in process: mkimage must not need a shell. */
+		if (copyfile(params->imagefile, tmpfile) < 0)
+			goto err_system;
+		*cmd = '\0';
 	}
 	if (strlen(cmd) >= MKIMAGE_MAX_DTC_CMDLINE_LEN - 1) {
 		fprintf(stderr, "WARNING: command-line for FIT creation might be truncated and will probably fail.\n");
